  "text": "WHO Clinical Guidelines on Common Cold and Upper Respiratory Self-Care | Publisher: World \nSection 2: Respiratory Red Flags and Medical Escalation\nRed Flag Symptoms Requiring Immediate Medical Attention: - Difficulty\nbreathing, wheezing, or shortness of breath. - Persistent high fever\n(>38.5\u00b0C or 101.3\u00b0F) lasting more than 3 days. - Chest pain or pressure\nduring breathing or coughing. - Coughing up blood or thick discolored\nsputum. - Bluish lips or face (cyanosis).\nSpecial Considerations: Children, elderly individuals, and adults with\nasthma or COPD should seek early medical advice for respiratory symptoms.\nPage 2 of 4 | Official Educational Resource",
